ote having said that expats  lebanese living abroad did an amazing  job  voting in big numbers for for for the  opposition so this is this is where we  stand now obviously our our i mean our  main goal is the following  to be able to legislate to pass in laws  to stop the bleeding this is this is the  first thing we want to do  second thing the second thing we want to  do is actually  to  to uh to allow lebanese people to have  hope again to have faith in these  newcomers of the political scene this is  very very important it helps this is  positive energy we need to be there we  need to motivate people we need to  engage people we need to remind them  that the practice of politics can be  good that not everything linked to  politics is bad because they've seen bad  practices for the last three years as i  said earlier this is very very important  but obviously the priority stop the  bleeding talk to the ayat we need to be  able to although this is not the job of  the parliament but we need to be  informed about any negotiations  happening between the lebanese  government the imf lebanese government  the eu lebanese government the world  bank and so on and so forth so we have  to be able to tell the lebanese people  what is happening this is very very  important when i say we want to be the  voices this is not a populist rhetoric  this is really a new practice of  politics because that will help us at  some point regain the country we will  literally show the lebanese people  everything that is happening within the  lebanese political system all the  institutions this is very very important  so and we need their backing at some  point and but we need to get there we  need to get their approval and i think  that the only way to do that is to show  them how serious courageous and  people and we are and we have actually a  plan this is this is the only way out  for now michelle that being said how  slowly do you think the decision-making  will be if alliances aren't made and  there's no majority in parliament  this is this is i mean we don't want to  go into deadlocks again because  deadlocks have been been sort of  part of the political life because it's  a consolation of democracy as you  mentioned  in your report uh we need we need to  break that break that deadlock we need  to be able to legislate people voted for  us they expect us to be mature and when  i say mature  on the parliamentary at the  parliamentarian level it means we need  to pass in laws legislate and this means  we need to talk with other groups inside  parliament we need to get signatures  this is part of the parliamentary life  this is i mean no one has a much total  majority in terms of one block having  said that when it comes to sovereignty  issues i think our group uh the  newcomers i'm gonna call them that for  now i'm sure we're gonna find the name  at some point uh we're gonna  we're gonna be very very clear that this  country is not going to be able to  breathe again  unless  the only the only i mean the army and  the uh and the  official security services have control  and full monopoly over the weapons it  won't it won't  be able or not be able to  do it i mean to  come out of this uh of this mess without  without i mean  fixing that so it's a two-track it's a  two-track thing the economic collapse we  need to be there with the right laws  with with with really establishing what  we call the separation of power i mean  judiciary has to be independent  legislative body has to do its job and  there is the executive body  and the third job and lebanon part of  the problem is this practice it means  those three functions are we have a  fusion so we have members of parliament  who are ministers that they appoint  judges and this is why the system is not  breaking we gotta be there so that the  practice of democracy is healthy  well you certainly have a big job ahead  we wish you all the best michelle dewi  an elected member of parliament thank  you for 